### 5.4 The catalog-exposed set that must be archived or withdrawn (highest user impact)

These 20 are signed public packages **and** personas of another binary. Each needs one of: (a) archive the standalone repo and
withdraw the catalog entry, or (b) withdraw the persona from the host.

Table columns: Catalog package | Persona owner | Recommended action
- `oob3sum`, `oohash` | each other | designate one owner (RFC 0012 says `oob3sum`), archive the other, remove the reciprocal
  persona entry
- `oosha256`, `oosha512`, `oomd5` | `oob3sum` + `oohash` | archive all three repos; keep the persona tokens
- `oobase32`, `oobase58`, `oohex` | `oobase64` | archive the three; keep the persona tokens
- `oowget`, `oowget2`, `oofetch` | `oocurl` | archive the three; keep the persona tokens
- `oogzip`, `ooxz`, `oozstd`, `oounzip` | `ooarchive` / `oogzip` / `oo7z` | designate `ooarchive` as the archive host (RFC 0012
  §2.2 lists `ootar`, `oozstd`, `oogzip`, `ooxz`, `oozip` together); archive the four
- `oossl` | `oocert` | archive; keep the persona token
- `oomcp`, `oomcprun` | `openOODA-core/mcp` | **cross-org** — archive the tools repos, withdraw from `catalog/index`
- `ooripl` | `openOODA-core/tui` | **cross-org** — archive, withdraw
- `oonotify`, `oobar` | `openOODA-DE/oopanel` | **cross-org** — archive, withdraw, or rename the DE persona to `oopanel-notify`
  / `oopanel-bar`

### 5.5 The `oollama` anomaly

`openOODA-tools/oollama` is the **only** tools repo with **no catalog entry** (all 256 other tool repos are registered).
Meanwhile `openOODA-OS/ooaid/persona/anchor.oo:50-74` absorbs `oollama` as a persona ("Publish the canonical ooaid persona and
the absorbed oollama persona").

The catalog omission and the cross-org absorption are the same fact: the tool was absorbed into `ooaid` but the standalone repo
was never archived. `oollama` is therefore the one tools repo whose absence from the catalog is *correct* — every other tool's
presence is the anomaly.

